---
import Prose from '../layouts/Prose.astro';
import { site, url } from '../site';
---

<Prose title="Support" description="Answers to common questions about HostDeck, and how to get help.">
  <p>Answers to the questions people ask most. Can’t find yours? <a href={site.supportUrl}>Ask on GitHub</a>.</p>

  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>How do I open HostDeck?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Click its icon in the menu bar (a small stack of cards), or press <kbd>⌃⌥⌘S</kbd> from any app. You can change
          the shortcut, or turn it off, in Settings › General. In the menu, start typing to search, and press Return to
          open the top result.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Where are my hosts saved?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>In a single file on your Mac, with automatic backups next to it in a <code>Backups</code> folder:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Direct download: <code>~/Library/Application Support/HostDeck/hosts.json</code></li>
          <li>
            Mac App Store:
            <code>~/Library/Containers/net.othi.hostdeck/Data/Library/Application Support/HostDeck/hosts.json</code>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>Settings › Config File shows the file, and can import or export it.</p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>I used Shuttle. Can I bring my hosts?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Yes. The direct download imports <code>~/.shuttle.json</code> on first launch, keeping Shuttle’s ⌥⌘S shortcut.
          In the Mac App Store version, use Settings › Config File › Import and choose the file. The format stays
          Shuttle’s, so the file still works with Shuttle too.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Why does Terminal ask before connecting?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          The Mac App Store version opens SSH hosts as ssh:// links, and Terminal asks you to confirm links from other
          apps when the host isn’t in <code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code>. Click Allow. iTerm2 doesn’t ask; you can switch to
          it in Settings › General. With the HostDeck Terminal Script (see below), and in the direct download, HostDeck
          runs the command in Terminal itself, so there’s no question.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Can the Mac App Store version run any command, not just SSH hosts?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Yes, with the optional HostDeck Terminal Script. Apple’s rules don’t let an App Store app control Terminal by
          itself, but it may run a script you add to its Scripts folder. HostDeck includes one, as plain AppleScript you
          can read and change.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Choose Settings… from the HostDeck menu and open the Access tab.</li>
          <li>Click Open Scripts Folder, then drag the script shown in Settings into that folder.</li>
          <li>The first time a command runs, macOS asks whether HostDeck may control Terminal. Click OK.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          From then on every command runs in Terminal or iTerm2, with the profile and title you chose. iTerm2 opens new
          tabs; Terminal opens new windows, because it has no scripting command for tabs. To stop using the script, move
          it from the Scripts folder to the Bin.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do I show the hosts from my ~/.ssh/config?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          Turn on “Show hosts from ~/.ssh/config” in Settings › General. The Mac App Store version asks you once to allow
          access to your <code>.ssh</code> folder.
        </p>
        <p>
          To organise them, add a comment on a line under a <code>Host</code> line: <code># hostdeck.name Folder/Name</code>
          puts the host in a folder, and <code># hostdeck.environment prod</code> labels it Production.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>How do Production labels work?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          In Manage Hosts, give a host or a whole folder the Production, Staging or Development label. Labelled hosts get
          a coloured dot. In Settings › General › Production hosts, choose what happens before a Production host opens:
          ask to confirm, require Touch ID or your password, or nothing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Which apps open SFTP and FTP hosts?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          In Manage Hosts, choose Type › File transfer and enter the server. HostDeck opens it in Transmit, ForkLift,
          Cyberduck, Mountain Duck, Commander One or FileZilla, or Finder for FTP downloads. Pick one in Settings ›
          General › File transfer, or let HostDeck choose. To browse an SSH host’s files, hold ⌥ on it in the menu and choose
          Browse Files (SFTP).
        </p>
        <p>
          The free FileZilla client doesn’t accept links from other apps, so the direct download starts FileZilla with the
          address, and the Mac App Store version copies the link for FileZilla’s Quickconnect bar.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <details>
      <summary>Does HostDeck store my passwords?</summary>
      <div>
        <p>
          No. ssh signs in with your keys or asks for a password itself, and file-transfer apps keep passwords in their
          own keychain. See the <a href={url('privacy/')}>privacy policy</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>

  <div class="contact">
    <h2>Still stuck?</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={site.supportUrl}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>. Mention your macOS version and HostDeck version (HostDeck menu
      › About HostDeck), and which edition you use.
    </p>
  </div>
</Prose>
